//? Title: HMAC-SHA-256: two keyed hashes, exact fixtures and measured sensitivity
//? Subtitle: Actual intermediate bytes, full-range bit distances and one-shot memory costs
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A shared-key message authentication code binds a byte string to a key.
HMAC-SHA-256 forms an inner keyed hash and then hashes its digest under a
different keyed pad. We begin with a one-byte key and message, exposing actual
intermediate bytes from the producing execution. All seven RFC4231 cases extend
the original two reference examples. The original transfer-message demonstration
and all 136 single-key-bit changes are retained, with a full 0..256 distance
histogram that preserves the observed extrema. Padding boundaries and long keys
explain the selected SHA work and the implementation's one-shot memory cost. The
sensitivity experiment describes these fixtures; it is not a proof of security.
)

\toc

> One byte of key, two different pads

Use the public key byte ASCII K (hex 4b) and message byte ASCII A (hex 41).
SHA-256 has a 64-byte input block and 32-byte digest. Zero-pad the one-byte key
to 64 bytes. Its first inner-pad byte is 4b XOR36 =7d; the other 63 are 36. The
first outer-pad byte is 4b XOR5c =17; the others are 5c. Concatenating the inner
pad and message supplies 65 bytes to the inner SHA call. Concatenating the outer
pad and actual 32-byte inner digest supplies 96 bytes to the outer call.

This table and native diagram display actual bytes captured during one
execution; they do not recompute an illustrative substitute for the inner
digest. All displayed keys in this report are public fixtures. The educational
trace owns key-derived material and does not erase it securely.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hmac_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hmac_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256_trace("K", "A");
    std::print("| byte position | normalized key hex | inner pad hex | outer pad hex |\n| ---: | --- | --- " "| --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 4; ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {:02x} | {:02x} | {:02x} |\n", i, t.key_block[i],
                   t.inner_pad[i], t.outer_pad[i]);
    const auto inner =
        dm::algorithms::to_hex(t.inner_digest), tag =
        dm::algorithms::to_hex(t.run.tag);
    std::print("\nActual inner digest: {}\n\nActual complete tag: {}\n\nSHA calls {}; input bytes hashed " "{}; padded SHA blocks {}.\n",
               inner, tag, t.run.work.sha_calls, t.run.work.bytes_hashed,
               t.run.work.padded_blocks);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(65, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 4.6;
    style.height = 2.8;
    style.font_size = 21;
    d.node("key", {0, 0}, "Key block\n4b 00 ... 00\n64 bytes", style).node(
        "inner", {7, 0},
        std::format("Inner SHA-256\n65 input bytes\ndigest {}...",
                    inner.substr(0, 8)), style).node("outer", {14, 0},
                                                     std::format(
                                                     "Outer SHA-256\n96 input bytes\ntag {}...",
                                                     tag.substr(0, 8)), style).edge("key", "inner", "XOR 36; append A").edge("inner", "outer", "outer pad + digest").text({7, 3}, "Distinct pads: first bytes 7d (inner), 17 (outer)", 24).save_svg("build/hmac_tiny.svg");
    if (t.inner_pad[0] != 0x7d || t.outer_pad[0] != 0x17 ||
        t.run.work.bytes_hashed != 161)
        throw std::runtime_error("tiny pads");
}
```
