\caption(Key normalization, pad bytes and the nested digest belong to one producing HMAC. The outer pad is made from the same normalized key.)
\alttext(Three nodes show the zero-padded one-byte key and the actual inner and outer SHA input lengths and digest prefixes.)

> Derive the construction, with long-key normalization

Let B=64 bytes and H=SHA-256. Define a normalized key block K0 by hashing K
first when its length exceeds B, then padding the resulting 32 bytes with zeros
to B. For keys of length at most B, pad the supplied bytes directly. Exactly 64
bytes is not the prehash branch. Then

$$I=H((K_0\mathbin{\operatorname{XOR}}36_{hex}^{64})\Vert m),\qquad T=H((K_0\mathbin{\operatorname{XOR}}5c_{hex}^{64})\Vert I).$$
\alttext(The inner digest hashes the inner key pad and message. The final tag hashes the different outer key pad and the complete inner digest.)

The exponent 64 means repetition of a byte, not exponentiation. Concatenation
uses raw bytes, including zeros and values above 127. The outer input always has
96 bytes. The API returns all 32 tag bytes; hexadecimal formatting produces 64
lowercase characters.

A plain public hash supplies no shared-key authentication. The naive
secret-prefix hash H(K||m) also has a length-extension problem for
Merkle–Damgard hashes: its published digest can serve as a chaining state for an
extended padded input. HMAC's outer digest is over the different keyed pad and a
fixed-length inner digest; extending that outer input does not construct the
required tag of an extended message. This explains the structural distinction,
while a security argument still depends on assumptions about the hash and key.
Neither reference-vector agreement nor a finite sensitivity plot proves
unforgeability.

> All seven RFC4231 examples

The original RFC cases 1 and 2 are retained. Cases 3 and 4 supply repeated
binary message bytes; case 4's key contains bytes 01 through 19 hex. Cases 6 and
7 use 131-byte keys, exercising prehashing. Case 5 publishes only the leading
128 bits. Its row checks exactly that prefix, while the implementation still
returns a full 256-bit tag.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hmac_rfc.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hmac_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const auto cases = dm::algorithms::hmac_rfc4231_cases();
    std::vector<double> x, blocks, calls;
    std::print("| RFC case | key bytes | message bytes | key prehashed | compared bits | match |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | --- | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &c : cases) {
        const auto t = dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256_trace(c.key, c.message);
        const auto full = dm::algorithms::to_hex(t.run.tag);
        const bool match =
            full.substr(0, c.expected_hex.size()) == c.expected_hex;
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", c.case_number,
                   c.key.size(), c.message.size(), t.run.key_hashed,
                   4 * c.expected_hex.size(), match);
        x.push_back(c.case_number);
        blocks.push_back(t.run.work.padded_blocks);
        calls.push_back(t.run.work.sha_calls);
        if (!match)
            throw std::runtime_error("RFC4231 mismatch");
    }
    for (const auto &c : cases)
        std::print("\nCase {} actual full tag: {}\n", c.case_number,
                   dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256_hex(c.key, c.message));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, blocks, "padded blocks", {37, 99, 235}, 7.).points(
        x, calls, "SHA calls", {220, 38, 38}, 5.).title("RFC4231 cases: actual SHA invocations and derived padded blocks").x_label("RFC case number").y_label("selected count").y_limits(0, 12).save_svg("build/hmac_rfc.svg");
}
```
