\caption(All 136 distances are shown on the full possible scale; the exact histogram includes its observed 155-bit maximum rather than folding it into an endpoint bin.)
\alttext(The left panel shows every key-bit perturbation; the right shows all 257 exact distance bins, with observed mass concentrated near 128.)

The complete experiment is bounded before baseline hashing: default 4096 key-bit
flips and one million combined key/message input bytes, with zero disabling
either policy. Empty keys have no bits to flip: they still produce a baseline,
but return an empty distance vector and has_flips=false, with zero summary
sentinels. These limits throw rather than sample or truncate. The helper stores
8k distances and a fixed histogram; it performs 8k+1 complete HMAC evaluations
for a k-byte key. Their cost is O((8k+1)(k+m+1)) time and O(k+m+8k+1) additional
working/output storage.

> Padding boundaries, selected work and exact admission

SHA-256 appends one marker byte, zeros and an eight-byte original bit length.
For an L-byte SHA input, its padded block count is

$$b(L)=\left\lfloor\frac{L+8}{64}\right\rfloor+1.$$
\alttext(The appended marker and eight-byte length require another block whenever fewer than nine bytes remain.)

With no key prehash, HMAC supplies 64+m bytes to the inner hash and 96 to the
outer hash, so total padded blocks are b(64+m)+2. This jumps between message
lengths 55 and 56, then repeats every 64 bytes. For k>64, add b(k) blocks and a
third SHA call. The unpadded bytes hashed are 160+m+(k if k>64 else 0). These
are call-boundary counters and padding-derived totals, not measured timing, an
internal SHA trace or total allocation traffic.

The existing one-shot SHA implementation copies its input and appends padding.
HMAC also constructs concatenated pad/message inputs. Therefore ordinary
execution has O(k+m+1) time and O(k+m+1) additional storage, not the constant
working memory of a streaming implementation. The fixed intermediate trace adds
256 byte values beyond its ordinary run result, plus metadata/alignment; it
retains no message copy or round history. All arrays own their bytes. Complete
input caps count original key plus message bytes once, not memory use. Legacy
tag/hex functions have no optional policy cap; representable length/counter
checks still apply.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hmac_boundaries.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/hmac_analysis.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, short_blocks, long_blocks;
    std::print("| message bytes | key bytes | SHA calls | unpadded bytes hashed | padded blocks |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t m : {0, 1, 54, 55, 56, 63, 64, 118, 119, 120, 127, 128}) {
        const std::string message(m, 'x');
        x.push_back(m);
        for (std::size_t k : {64, 65}) {
            const auto r =
                dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256_run(std::string(k, 'k'), message);
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", m, k, r.work.sha_calls,
                       r.work.bytes_hashed, r.work.padded_blocks);
            (k == 64 ? short_blocks : long_blocks).push_back(
                r.work.padded_blocks);
        }
    }
    const auto exact = dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256_run("k", "m", {2});
    bool inputcap = false, flipcap = false;
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256_trace("k", "m", {1});
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        inputcap = true;
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::hmac_key_avalanche("k", "m", 7);
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        flipcap = true;
    }
    const auto empty = dm::algorithms::hmac_key_avalanche("", "");
    const std::string binary_key("\0\xff", 2), binary_message("a\0b", 3);
    std::print("\nExact 2-byte input accepted {}; one-short input rejected {}; one-short flip limit rejected " "{}; empty-key has flips {}.\n\nEmpty key/message tag: {}\n\nBinary key/message tag: {}\n",
               exact.tag == dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256("k", "m"), inputcap,
               flipcap, empty.has_flips, dm::algorithms::to_hex(empty.baseline),
               dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256_hex(binary_key, binary_message));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, short_blocks, "64-byte key", {37, 99, 235}, 6.).points(
        x, long_blocks, "65-byte key", {220, 38, 38}, 5.).title(
        "Exact padding thresholds and the key-prehash branch").x_label(
        "message bytes").y_label("derived total padded blocks").y_limits(
        0, 11).save_svg("build/hmac_boundaries.svg");
    if (!inputcap || !flipcap || empty.has_flips ||
        empty.baseline != dm::algorithms::hmac_sha256("", ""))
        throw std::runtime_error("boundary controls");
}
```
