\caption(Exactly 64 key bytes avoid prehashing;65 key bytes add two padded blocks for the prehash. Message thresholds are observed rather than interpolated into a smooth cost curve.)
\alttext(Actual call-boundary padded block totals jump at message lengths 56 and 120, with a constant extra prehash cost for the 65-byte key.)

> Validation and primary references

Native sanitizer tests check actual normalized keys, pads, inner/final digests
and work across key/message boundaries, all seven published fixtures, binary
inputs, empty keys, exact/one-short limits and independent ownership. Frozen
pre-conversion tags and independent Python hmac/hashlib comparisons verify
complete tags and captured intermediates. All 136 original key changes are
independently checked. Documentation examples, symbol hovers, real MEP
executions, inspected native figures and byte-checked image/result exports are
required before retiring the legacy report.

The construction follows [RFC2104, HMAC: Keyed-Hashing for Message
Authentication](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2104.html), H. Krawczyk, M.
Bellare and R. Canetti,1997. The public conformance data follow [RFC4231,
section 4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4231.html#section-4), M.
Nystrom,2005. The case 5 prefix check is explicitly distinct from full 256-bit
conformance. Neither the plots nor this educational implementation claim a
security certification or a production verification protocol.
